  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 928 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nA relatively calm overnight period is progressing across the Mid-\nSouth with temperatures currently in the upper 40s to 50s. Areas\nof dense fog have already begun to develop to our east behind the\nfrontal boundary that pushed through earlier in the day, with\nsome patchy fog already developing across north MS. Conditions do\nappear favorable for dense fog over our area overnight, with\nsaturated grounds, calm winds, and clear skies. The biggest\nquestion for our area will be how widespread the dense fog will\nbecome. REFS guidance has a 40% to 60% chance of widespread dense\nfog development, where some other hi-res guidance keeps this area\nmainly confined to north MS. As such, going to error on the side\nof caution for now with a Dense Fog Advisory only encompassing\nnorth MS. However, if trends do change, then an expansion across\nthe entire area may be warranted. Any dense fog that does develop\nwill begin to mix out following sunrise. Dry conditions are\nexpected on Thursday, with high temperatures remaining above-\nnormal in the upper 60s to low 70s.\n\nA dry, reinforcing cold front will sweep through the region\novernight Thursday into early Friday, allowing for temperatures\nto fall back even further heading into the upcoming weekend.\nWhile high temperatures will remain close to normal on Friday in\nthe 60s to low 70s across our far southeast, high temperatures\nwill dip below normal by Saturday. High temperatures on Saturday\nwill likely be in the upper 50s to low 60s, with temperatures in\nthe low to mid 60s on Sunday. With the cooler temperatures,\novernight lows will likely fall into the 30s across the entire\nMid-South both Saturday and Sunday. While not expected to be\nwidespread, there is a 20% to 30% chance Saturday morning and a\n30% to 50% chance Sunday morning for some locations to see their\nfirst freeze of the season. The best probabilities are generally\nacross northeast AR, the Missouri Bootheel, and West TN. By\nMonday and into next week, a gradual warm up is expected with\nhigh temperatures returning to near-normal in the low to mid 60s.\nGuidance continues to favor a dry forecast through next week.",
